Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["", "SlideShowNavigationBar",
 CellTags->
  "SlideShowHeader",ExpressionUUID->"98c68787-e87d-844c-86d1-5ce30a055c84"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["3 Sensitivity Analysis", "Section",
 PageBreakAbove->True,
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.694341537930465*^9, 3.694341550254487*^9}, {
   3.694342355079833*^9, 3.694342363410248*^9}, 3.6943428651189747`*^9, {
   3.6943430193857985`*^9, 3.694343019923829*^9}, {3.6943431695433865`*^9, 
   3.694343174763685*^9}, 3.6943455517965236`*^9, 3.9200052201975765`*^9, {
   3.991661420697618*^9, 
   3.9916614207486773`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"b7395a63-f00d-488c-8531-\
cc9901abc9ed"],

Cell["\<\
The previous chapter introduced several algorithmic approaches for \
identifying narcissistic numbers. These approaches differ in how they handle \
the search space and how much redundant computation they avoid. However, \
runtime performance is not only determined by the algorithm itself, but also \
by input size, digit length, implementation details, and the use of \
parallelization.

The purpose of this sensitivity analysis is therefore to examine how strongly \
the computation time reacts to changes in selected parameters. In this \
notebook, the most relevant parameter is the digit dimension of the search \
interval. Increasing the number of digits directly increases the number of \
candidate numbers in the brute force approach. In contrast, the digit \
permutation class approach reacts differently, because it operates on digit \
compositions instead of individual numbers.

Furthermore, the sensitivity analysis investigates whether parallelization \
consistently improves runtime. Since parallelization introduces overhead \
through kernel startup, task distribution, and result collection, it is not \
guaranteed to be faster for small search spaces. The analysis therefore helps \
to identify under which conditions each approach becomes efficient.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.991277372497469*^9},ExpressionUUID->"23072a7d-2268-414e-99a0-\
86da41fda409"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["", "SlideShowNavigationBar",
 CellTags->
  "SlideShowHeader",ExpressionUUID->"eaa28263-5b68-fe4f-9c8a-1035541b6c0b"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["3.1 Sensitivity to Search Dimensions", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.6943454921732183`*^9, 3.694345497976429*^9}, 
   3.6943455631221437`*^9, {3.991277389340275*^9, 3.9912773942256546`*^9}, {
   3.991661422955941*^9, 
   3.991661423030471*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"e266913f-bfaa-480f-bc1f-\
1495575a462c"],

Cell["\<\
The first sensitivity parameter is the digit length of the search interval. \
This parameter has a direct effect on the size of the search space. In the \
brute force approach, every number in the interval has to be checked \
individually. Therefore, the number of candidates grows exponentially with \
the digit length.

For a fixed digit length d, there are 9 * 10^(d - 1) positive d-digit \
numbers. This explains why the brute force approach quickly becomes \
infeasible for larger dimensions.

The digit permutation class approach is less sensitive to the raw number of \
candidates. Instead of evaluating every number, it evaluates possible digit \
compositions. The number of digit compositions of length d corresponds to the \
number of non-negative integer solutions of x0 + x1 + ... + x9 = d, which is \
Binomial[d + 9, 9]. Although this number also increases, it grows much more \
slowly than the full brute force search space.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.6551323318137064`*^9, 3.655132346618133*^9}, {
  3.6558834788297663`*^9, 3.655883482105772*^9}, {3.655883581805547*^9, 
  3.655883589683561*^9}, {3.6558837346868153`*^9, 3.655883735919217*^9}, {
  3.694341574232729*^9, 3.694341596852769*^9}, {3.694342244717239*^9, 
  3.694342247462844*^9}, {3.694342333660995*^9, 3.694342343083412*^9}, {
  3.6943424202555304`*^9, 3.694342429299047*^9}, {3.6943424762647333`*^9, 
  3.694342484642213*^9}, {3.694342535512122*^9, 3.694342552269081*^9}, {
  3.6943425832578535`*^9, 3.694342597036641*^9}, {3.694342646046445*^9, 
  3.694342765915301*^9}, {3.694342880577859*^9, 3.694342892973568*^9}, {
  3.920005136849468*^9, 3.9200051373384686`*^9}, {3.991277386556095*^9, 
  3.991277387646248*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"6cec9e2c-a51b-4d44-b524-\
aaa208b63e30"],

Cell["\<\
The following table compares the number of candidates that have to be \
evaluated by the brute force approach with the number of digit permutation \
classes for each digit length. The reduction factor shows how many times \
larger the brute force search space is compared to the digit class search \
space.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.99155294622958*^9},ExpressionUUID->"56dd00cd-545a-4549-8866-d3a8a8246ced"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{" ", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"Clear", "[", 
    RowBox[{"candidateCount", ",", "digitClassCount"}], "]"}], 
   "\[IndentingNewLine]", "\n", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"candidateCount", "[", "d_", "]"}], ":=", 
    RowBox[{"If", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"d", "==", "1"}], ",", "10", ",", 
      RowBox[{"9", "*", 
       RowBox[{"10", "^", 
        RowBox[{"(", 
         RowBox[{"d", "-", "1"}], ")"}]}]}]}], "]"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]",
    "\n", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"digitClassCount", "[", "d_", "]"}], ":=", 
    RowBox[{"Binomial", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"d", "+", "9"}], ",", "9"}], "]"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "\n", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"complexityRows", "=", 
     RowBox[{"Table", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{"d", ",", 
         RowBox[{"candidateCount", "[", "d", "]"}], ",", 
         RowBox[{"digitClassCount", "[", "d", "]"}], ",", 
         RowBox[{"NumberForm", "[", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"N", "[", 
            RowBox[{
             RowBox[{"candidateCount", "[", "d", "]"}], "/", 
             RowBox[{"digitClassCount", "[", "d", "]"}]}], "]"}], ",", 
           RowBox[{"{", 
            RowBox[{"Infinity", ",", "2"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{"d", ",", "1", ",", "maxdimension"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}], ";"}],
    "\[IndentingNewLine]", "\n", 
   RowBox[{"Grid", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"Prepend", "[", 
      RowBox[{"complexityRows", ",", 
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{"\"\<Digit length\>\"", ",", "\"\<Brute force candidates\>\"",
          ",", "\"\<Digit classes\>\"", ",", "\"\<Reduction factor\>\""}], 
        "}"}]}], "]"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"Frame", "->", "All"}]}], "]"}]}]}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.6943456316374636`*^9, 3.9912774064308224`*^9},
 CellLabel->"In[50]:=",ExpressionUUID->"ff44551d-0964-41c8-b307-9984cd019dbd"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[GridBox[{
    {"\<\"Digit length\"\>", "\<\"Brute force candidates\"\>", "\<\"Digit \
classes\"\>", "\<\"Reduction factor\"\>"},
    {"1", "10", "10", 
     TagBox[
      InterpretationBox[
       StyleBox["\<\"1.00\"\>",
        ShowStringCharacters->False],
       1.,
       AutoDelete->True],
      NumberForm[#, {
        DirectedInfinity[1], 2}]& ]},
    {"2", "90", "55", 
     TagBox[
      InterpretationBox[
       StyleBox["\<\"1.64\"\>",
        ShowStringCharacters->False],
       1.6363636363636365`,
       AutoDelete->True],
      NumberForm[#, {
        DirectedInfinity[1], 2}]& ]},
    {"3", "900", "220", 
     TagBox[
      InterpretationBox[
       StyleBox["\<\"4.09\"\>",
        ShowStringCharacters->False],
       4.090909090909091,
       AutoDelete->True],
      NumberForm[#, {
        DirectedInfinity[1], 2}]& ]},
    {"4", "9000", "715", 
     TagBox[
      InterpretationBox[
       StyleBox["\<\"12.59\"\>",
        ShowStringCharacters->False],
       12.587412587412587`,
       AutoDelete->True],
      NumberForm[#, {
        DirectedInfinity[1], 2}]& ]},
    {"5", "90000", "2002", 
     TagBox[
      InterpretationBox[
       StyleBox["\<\"44.96\"\>",
        ShowStringCharacters->False],
       44.955044955044954`,
       AutoDelete->True],
      NumberForm[#, {
        DirectedInfinity[1], 2}]& ]},
    {"6", "900000", "5005", 
     TagBox[
      InterpretationBox[
       StyleBox["\<\"179.82\"\>",
        ShowStringCharacters->False],
       179.82017982017982`,
       AutoDelete->True],
      NumberForm[#, {
        DirectedInfinity[1], 2}]& ]}
   },
   AutoDelete->False,
   GridBoxFrame->{"Columns" -> {{True}}, "Rows" -> {{True}}},
   GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}}],
  "Grid"]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.9912779783420715`*^9, 3.9912857232843895`*^9, 
  3.9912875705592785`*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[54]=",ExpressionUUID->"705a32a5-ae85-244b-8cad-b8ab9fee7658"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
The table shows that both approaches start with the same number of objects \
for one-digit numbers. However, as the digit length increases, the number of \
brute force candidates grows much faster than the number of digit classes. \
The reduction factor therefore increases with each additional digit, showing \
that the digit permutation class approach becomes increasingly more efficient \
for larger search dimensions. This explains why reducing the search space is \
more important than simply evaluating the original brute force search faster.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.991553116731838*^9, 3.991556421686266*^9, 
  3.9915568597344074`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"c74019d5-3350-d04c-8e9e-\
2849b166f3df"],

Cell["\<\
The following logarithmic plot visualizes how the search space changes with \
increasing digit length. It compares the growth of brute force candidates \
with the growth of digit permutation classes.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9915564481649036`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"6b48655d-39a6-9c42-b380-\
c0a27987a321"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"ListLogPlot", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"Table", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{"d", ",", 
         RowBox[{"candidateCount", "[", "d", "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{"d", ",", "1", ",", "maxdimension"}], "}"}]}], "]"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"Table", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{"d", ",", 
         RowBox[{"digitClassCount", "[", "d", "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{"d", ",", "1", ",", "maxdimension"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}], "}"}],
    ",", 
   RowBox[{"PlotLegends", "->", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"\"\<Brute force candidates\>\"", 
      ",", "\"\<Digit permutation classes\>\""}], "}"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"AxesLabel", "->", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"\"\<Digit length\>\"", ",", "\"\<Number of objects\>\""}], 
     "}"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
   "PlotLabel", 
    "->", "\"\<Sensitivity of search space to digit length\>\""}]}], 
  "]"}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.991277420052744*^9},
 CellLabel->"In[55]:=",ExpressionUUID->"c6a7938d-641e-4c47-9933-22cca05418ab"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TemplateBox[{
   GraphicsBox[{{}, 
     InterpretationBox[{
       TagBox[{
         TagBox[{
           RGBColor[0.24, 0.6, 0.8], 
           PointSize[0.012833333333333334`], 
           AbsoluteThickness[2], 
           
           PointBox[{{1., 2.302585092994046}, {2., 4.499809670330265}, {3., 
            6.802394763324311}, {4., 9.104979856318357}, {5., 
            11.407564949312402`}, {6., 13.710150042306449`}}]}, 
          Annotation[#, "Charting`Private`Tag#1"]& ], 
         TagBox[{
